course: physics
lesson: 11
title: Floating and sinking: the push of the water
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Water pushes up on anything you put in it, and the push is as big as the weight of the water the thing moves out of the way.
explain: Climb into the bath and the water rises: your body has pushed water aside, and that water pushes back up on you.
explain: Something floats if it is lighter than the same size of water: wood floats, a stone sinks, and a hollow steel ship floats because it is mostly air inside.
explain: Shape matters too: a ball of clay sinks, but the same clay pressed into a little boat moves aside much more water and floats.
explain: Salty water pushes up harder than fresh water, which is why you float more easily in the sea.
fact: Water pushes up on anything in it, with a force as big as the weight of the water it moves aside.
fact: Things float if they are lighter than the same size of water; wood is light for its size and stone is heavy for its size.
fact: A big beach ball moves aside lots of water but weighs little, so it is hard to hold under and shoots back up.
fact: The water in the bath rises when you get in, by as much water as the part of you under the surface.
fact: Archimedes is said to have noticed this in his bath and shouted "Eureka!", but the story may not be true.
fact: A steel ship floats because it is hollow and full of air, so the water it moves aside weighs more than the ship; squashed into a lump, it would sink.
fact: The same clay sinks as a ball but floats as a boat, because the boat shape moves aside more water.
fact: A loaded ship sits lower in the water, and a line painted on its side shows how deep it may safely sit.
fact: Salty water is heavier than fresh water, so it pushes up harder, and in the very salty Dead Sea people float sitting up.
fact: An egg sinks in tap water but floats when lots of salt is stirred in, and a rotten egg floats even in fresh water because gas has built up inside.
fact: You float best lying back with your lungs full of air, and breathing out makes you sink a little.
fact: Muscle sinks more than fat, so very sporty people may float less well.
fact: A life jacket is full of foam or air, adding lots of size but little weight, and a good one turns you face up even if you cannot swim; it also keeps up a good swimmer who gets tired, cold or hurt.
exercise: Why does wood float and a stone sink? | Wood is lighter than the same size of water; stone is heavier | Wood has air holes and stone has none at all | Water pulls stones down and pushes wood up | Stones are cold and wood is warm
exercise: How big is the water's push up on something? | As big as the weight of the water it moves aside | As big as the weight of all the water in the whole pond | Always the same for every object | As big as the thing's own height
exercise: Why is a big beach ball so hard to push under water? | It moves aside lots of water but weighs little | It is full of very heavy air that is squashed inside it | The water is sticky on plastic | Gravity does not pull beach balls
exercise: The bigger the ball, the ... | Harder it is to hold under | Easier it is to hold under | Faster it sinks | Heavier the water gets
exercise: Why does the bath water rise when you get in? | Your body pushes water out of the way | Your body heat makes the water swell and grow bigger | The water soaks into your skin | The tap turns on by itself
exercise: Who is said to have shouted "Eureka!" in his bath? | Archimedes | Galileo | Isaac Newton | Julius Caesar
exercise: How can a ship made of steel float? | It is hollow and full of air, so it moves aside lots of water | Steel is lighter than water | The ship's engines hold it up | Sea water is heavier than steel
exercise: What would happen if a steel ship were squashed into a solid lump? | It would sink like a nail | It would float even higher | It would float on its side | It would float halfway down
exercise: Why does a clay boat float when a clay ball sinks? | The boat shape moves aside more water | The boat is much lighter than the ball of clay | The ball is made of heavier clay | Water does not touch the boat
exercise: Why does a loaded ship sit lower in the water? | It is heavier, so it must move aside more water | The cargo pulls the water down underneath it as it is loaded | The water gets lighter around it | The ship gets smaller when loaded
exercise: What is the line painted on a ship's side for? | To show how deep it may safely sit | To show the ship's name to other boats | To make the ship look faster and smarter | To show the sailors where the fish swim
exercise: Why is it easier to float in the sea? | Salty water is heavier, so it pushes up harder | The waves keep lifting you up | Sea water is warmer than pool water | The sea is deeper than a pool
exercise: How can salt make an egg float? | It makes the water heavier than the egg | It makes the egg lighter by soaking into its shell | It fills the egg with air | It makes the shell harder
exercise: Why does a rotten egg float even in fresh water? | Gas has built up inside it | It has lost its shell in the water | It has slowly turned into stone | It is full of salt from the fridge
exercise: Why does breathing in help you float? | Air in your lungs makes you lighter for your size | Air makes your body heavier | You can see better with air in | Breathing pushes the water away
exercise: Why should you wear a life jacket in a boat, even if you can swim? | It keeps you up even when you are tired or hurt | It makes you swim faster | It keeps you dry in the boat | It is only for people who cannot swim
test: Lily's mum says steel always sinks, so how come ships float? | A ship is hollow and full of air | Ships are made of light wood under the paint | Sea water holds up all metal | Ships float on their engines
test: In the Dead Sea, what can people do? | Float sitting up, reading a newspaper | Sink straight down to the bottom like a stone | Walk on the water | Breathe under the water
test: An egg sinks in your glass. You stir in lots of salt. What happens? | It floats | It sinks faster | It cracks | It dissolves
test: lol my brother is all muscle and he sinks like a brick in the pool, why | Muscle sinks more than fat | Muscle is full of air | He breathes in too much | Pools are saltier for some people
test: What would happen if you breathed all the air out while floating? | You would sink a little | You would float even higher than before | You would shoot straight up out of the water | Nothing at all would change
test: You put marbles one by one into a clay boat. What happens at last? | It sinks once it gets too heavy | It floats higher and higher in the water | It turns into a ball | The marbles float out
test: In a cartoon a man grabs a big stone and floats across the river on it. What is wrong? | A stone is heavier than the same size of water, so it sinks | Nothing, stones float well | The stone would fly up | Stones float in rivers but not the sea
test: You hold a beach ball under water and let go. What does it do? | It shoots back up | It stays where it is | It sinks to the bottom | It floats off sideways
test: Why does a life jacket help even someone who cannot swim? | It turns you face up and keeps you afloat | It teaches you how to swim as you go along | It makes the water shallower | It makes you lighter than air
test: Which floats? | A cork | A key | A marble | A coin
test: Your bath is full to the brim. What happens when you get in? | Water spills over the side | The water level goes down a bit | Nothing at all | The water gets hotter
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the upward force exerted by water upon an immersed body? | It is equal to the weight of the water displaced | It is equal to the weight of the body in every case | It is independent of the volume of the body | It is exerted only upon bodies that float
exam: Identify the principal reason for which a vessel constructed of steel remains afloat. | Its hollow form displaces a weight of water exceeding its own | Steel is of lesser density than water | The engines exert a continual upward force | Salt water exerts no downward force upon metal
exam: It is given that a quantity of salt is dissolved in water in which an egg has sunk. Ascertain the consequence. | The egg rises to float | The egg descends more rapidly | The egg becomes heavier | The water becomes lighter
exam: Determine the function of the line marked upon the hull of a merchant vessel. | It indicates the greatest depth to which the vessel may safely be immersed | It indicates the port at which the vessel was registered | It indicates the speed that the vessel may attain | It indicates the position of the engines
